arXiv:2606.29656v1 [math.AG] 28 Jun 2026</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Hannah Larson
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">In this expository article, we present on state-of-the art results regarding three closely related invariants of moduli spaces of curves: their Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts over finite fields. We study the moduli space <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, parameterizing smooth genus <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> curves with <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> marked points, as well as its compactification by stable curves <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. After explaining the relationship between these different invariants, we survey what is know regarding the following related questions: When are the Chow rings tautological? When are the cohomology groups tautological? And when are the point counts over fields of size <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> given by a polynomial in <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>?</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First conceived of by Riemann in 1857 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>, the moduli space <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> of smooth genus <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> curves is a central object of study in algebraic geometry. Its compactification <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, parameterizing stable genus <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> curves, was introduced by Deligne and Mumford in 1969 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>.
The points in the boundary of <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to nodal curves with finite automorphism group. Such curves are built by gluing together smooth curves along marked points. It is therefore natural to study all together the system of moduli spaces <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> parameterizing smooth genus <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> curves with <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> marked points and their compactifications <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> parameterizing stable curves of genus <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> marked points.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There are natural maps between these moduli spaces corresponding to gluing</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},n_{1}+1}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},n_{2}+1}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1}+g_{2},n_{1}+n_{2}}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,n+2}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},n_{1}+1}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},n_{2}+1}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1}+g_{2},n_{1}+n_{2}}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,n+2}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and forgetting marked points</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n+1}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n+1}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">The boundary of <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is stratified according to the topological type of the stable curve, which is encoded in a dual graph <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. For example the topological type pictured on the left below corresponds to the dual graph on the right.</p><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.29656v1/graph.svg" id="S1.p2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" width="399" height="144"></object>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given a dual graph <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, we define</p>
<table id="S1.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}\mathrel{\mathchoice{\vbox{\hbox{$\displaystyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\textstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle:$}}}{=}}\prod_{v\in\Gamma}\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{v},n_{v}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0.278em">:</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo></mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}\mathrel{\mathchoice{\vbox{\hbox{$\displaystyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\textstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle:$}}}{=}}\prod_{v\in\Gamma}\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{v},n_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where the product ranges over the vertices of <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is the genus of the vertex, and <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of half-edges incident to the vertex.
The closure of the stratum of curves with topological type <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is the image of the gluing map</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\Gamma}\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\Gamma}\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">given by gluing marked points that are joined by an edge in <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. We refer the reader to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, Chapter X]</cite> for further definitions and background on stable curves.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this article, we study different aspects of the geometry of these moduli spaces of curves, as captured by three closely related invariants.
The Chow ring, denoted <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math>, is an algebraic invariant, capturing information about the algebraic cycles and how they intersect each other.
The cohomology ring, denoted <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math>, is an invariant of the topological space formed by the complex points of the moduli space. Throughout, Chow and cohomology rings will be taken with rational coefficients.
Finally,
because these moduli spaces are defined over the integers, we may also consider arithmetic invariants. In particular, upon base changing to any finite field <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, the moduli space becomes a finite set of points, and we can count the number of such points, denoted <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\mathcal{M}(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\mathcal{M}(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          The moduli spaces in question are smooth Deligne–Mumford stacks. For such spaces, one can also define “stacky” point counts. However, these agree with the usual point counts of their coarse moduli spaces, see Section <a href="#S2.SS4" title="2.4. Comparing cohomology and point counts ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a> for more details.</span></span></span> One can ask to compute each of these invariants for <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. In Section <a href="#S2" title="2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we will explain how these different invariants relate to each other.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The natural maps (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>) and (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>) between moduli spaces of curves give rise to a distinguished collection of classes, called tautological classes, which will help to guide our study of their Chow and cohomology rings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The tautological subrings <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\subset A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\subset A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> are the smallest system of subrings closed under push forward along the gluing and forgetful maps. The tautological subring <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\subset A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\subset A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is the image of <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> under the restriction map <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\to A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\to A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For either <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the
tautological cohomology ring, denoted <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{*}(\mathcal{M})\subset H^{*}(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{*}(\mathcal{M})\subset H^{*}(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math>, is the image of <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> under the cycle class map <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M})\to H^{*}(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M})\to H^{*}(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Compared with the entire Chow or cohomology rings, the tautological subrings are relatively well-understood. They admit additive generators called <em id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">decorated boundary strata</em>. To define them, we first recall the kappa and psi classes. Let <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n+1}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n+1}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the map defined by forgetting the last marking and stabilizing. In fact, <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the universal curve over <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and comes
equipped with <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> disjoint sections</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{1},\ldots,\sigma_{n}\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n+1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{1},\ldots,\sigma_{n}\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">corresponding to the <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> markings.
We define
<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
          To see that the classes <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (and hence also <math id="footnote2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>) are tautological according to Definition <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Definition 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, we realize the section <math id="footnote2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as a gluing map
<math id="footnote2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,\{1,\ldots,\widehat{i},\ldots,n\}}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,\{p^{\prime},i,n+1\}}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}\colon\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,\{1,\ldots,\widehat{i},\ldots,n\}}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,\{p^{\prime},i,n+1\}}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>
which glues <math id="footnote2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="footnote2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Definition <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Definition 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, the class <math id="footnote2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{*}(\sigma_{i*}1)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{*}(\sigma_{i*}1)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> must be tautological. On the other hand, using the self-intersection formula, we have
<math id="footnote2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\sigma_{i*}1)^{2}=\sigma_{i*}(c_{1}(N_{\sigma_{i}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})/\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n+1})})=\sigma_{i*}(c_{1}(\sigma_{i}^{*}\omega_{f}^{\vee}))=\sigma_{i*}(-\psi_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>f</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sigma_{i*}1)^{2}=\sigma_{i*}(c_{1}(N_{\sigma_{i}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})/\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n+1})})=\sigma_{i*}(c_{1}(\sigma_{i}^{*}\omega_{f}^{\vee}))=\sigma_{i*}(-\psi_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The second equality above follows from identifying the normal bundle to the section with the restriction of the relative tangent bundle to the section. Finally, since <math id="footnote2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a section of <math id="footnote2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, we have
<math id="footnote2.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R^{1}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\ni f_{*}(\sigma_{i*}1)^{2}=f_{*}\sigma_{i*}(-\psi_{i})=-\psi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∋</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{1}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\ni f_{*}(\sigma_{i*}1)^{2}=f_{*}\sigma_{i*}(-\psi_{i})=-\psi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>,
showing that <math id="footnote2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological.</span></span></span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}=c_{1}(\sigma_{i}^{*}\omega_{f})\in A^{1}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\qquad\text{and}\qquad\kappa_{j}=f_{*}(\psi_{n+1}^{j+1})\in A^{j}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}=c_{1}(\sigma_{i}^{*}\omega_{f})\in A^{1}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\qquad\text{and}\qquad\kappa_{j}=f_{*}(\psi_{n+1}^{j+1})\in A^{j}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Given any stable graph <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, classes of the following form are tautological by Definition <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Definition 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>:</p>
<table id="S1.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\xi_{\Gamma*}\left(\prod_{v\in\Gamma}\mathrm{pr}_{v}^{*}(\text{monomial in $\psi_{i}$ and $\kappa_{j}$})\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>pr</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>monomial in </mtext><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext> and </mtext><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\Gamma*}\left(\prod_{v\in\Gamma}\mathrm{pr}_{v}^{*}(\text{monomial in $\psi_{i}$ and $\kappa_{j}$})\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p7.2" class="ltx_p">A class of the form (<a href="#S1.E4" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>) is called a <em id="S1.p7.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">decorated boundary stratum</em>, and can be represented by a decorated stable graph. For example, the decorated stable graph below represents a tautological class in <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{4}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{6,2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{4}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{6,2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p><img src="2606.29656v1/dec-strata.png" id="S1.p7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" width="130" height="76" alt="[Uncaptioned image]">
<p id="S1.p7.3" class="ltx_p">The name comes from the fact that <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\xi_{\Gamma*}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\Gamma*}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> is the fundamental class of a boundary stratum, but we also allow <math id="S1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> “decorations”. The graph <math id="S1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is allowed to be the trivial graph with one vertex of genus <math id="S1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, which recovers the usual <math id="S1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> classes.
Using excess intersection, an algorithm to express a product of decorated boundary strata as a sum of decorated boundary strata is given in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, Appendix A]</cite>. Consequently, one concludes that decorated boundary strata in fact form an additive set of generators for <math id="S1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">There is a systematic set of known relations among decorated boundary strata, known as Pixton’s relations, which are conjectured to be all relations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>. We refer the reader to the excellent survey article <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> for a description of these tautological relations, their history, and open questions about them.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Computing the full Chow ring, cohomology ring, or exact point counts of <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> are difficult problems which have only been answered in low genera. These low genus results are of interest in their own right, but also play a key role in answering more qualitative questions, in ranges where exact calculation of these invariants seems out of reach. In this article, we will present the current progress on the following related questions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For which <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the Chow/cohomology ring of <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by tautological classes?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S3" title="3. When are the Chow or cohomology rings tautological? ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we survey a large body of work on this question, which leaves it unanswered in a small, finite number of cases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">In our first question above, we fix <math id="S1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and ask if the entire Chow/cohomology ring is tautological. A more refined question would be to also fix a codimension in Chow or a cohomological degree. In Chow, there are relatively few results in this direction. It is known that <math id="S1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{1}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=\Pic(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Pic</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{1}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=\Pic(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological for all <math id="S1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, if <math id="S1.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is rationally connected, then the recent paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, Theorem 1.4]</cite> shows that <math id="S1.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{2}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{2}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological.
In Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Cohomology in low weights ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we will present progress on this question when fixing a cohomological degree.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For which <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k,g,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k,g,n</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> generated by tautological classes?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Going beyond tautological classes, in Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Cohomology in low weights ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> we also explain some recent results that describe odd cohomology groups of <math id="S1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. These groups are non-tautological, but admit decorated graph generators of a similar flavor.
An understanding of <math id="S1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> can also be used to shed light on the weight <math id="S1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> part of the compactly supported cohomology of the interior <math id="S1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, using Deligne’s weight spectral sequence. Much progress has been made in this direction as well, see Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1. Low weight compactly supported cohomology of 
          
            
              
              M
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  g
                  ,
                  n ‣ 4. Cohomology in low weights ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> for more detailed references.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, in Section <a href="#S5" title="5. Applications to point counts ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we connect results on the cohomology of <math id="S1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> to their point counts over finite fields. Our motivating qualitative question is the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 1.5</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For which <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g,n</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\mathcal{M}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\mathcal{M}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> a polynomial in <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p14" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">We also explain results of Bergström–Faber <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> where exact point counts are known in low genera, including in ranges where they are not polynomial.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Acknowledgements</h3>

<div id="S1.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">I am grateful to Samir Canning, Sam Payne, and an anonymous referee for valuable comments on an earlier version of this article.
This article was written during the period the author served as a Clay Research Fellow.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Relationships between the different invariants</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we collect results comparing the Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, we are interested in the following invariants, and aim to explain connections between them as indicated by edges below:</p><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.p1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="123.21" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-61.6px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 257.05 123.21" width="257.05"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,123.21) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(128.53,0) translate(0,61.6)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -128.53 -52.58)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 123.21)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 15.44)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 4.97 0) translate(31.37,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -25.41 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.67em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.34" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="50.83"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.p1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 114.01 0) translate(67.46,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -28.3 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.09em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.34" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="56.6"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.p1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 64.81)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 3.84 0) translate(32.5,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -26.54 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.84em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.34" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="53.08"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.p1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 72.68 0) translate(108.79,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -69.63 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:10.06em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.34" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="139.25"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.p1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces H^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\text{ or }H^{*}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> or </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces H^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\text{ or }H^{*}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 114.18)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(36.34,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -30.38 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.39em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.34" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="60.77"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.p1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 107.12 0) translate(74.35,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -35.19 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.09em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.34" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="70.38"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.p1.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\#\mathcal{M}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q}).}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\#\mathcal{M}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q}).}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -92.19 36.86 L -92.19 12.51"></path></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -60.54 49.62 L 18.41 49.62"></path></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 52.94 36.86 L 52.94 12.51"></path></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -92.19 -12.51 L -92.19 -36.86"></path></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -59.41 0.25 L -22.91 0.25"></path></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 52.94 -12.51 L 52.94 -36.86"></path></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -55.57 -49.12 L 11.52 -49.12"></path></g></g></svg></span>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth, <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> are dual to each other.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In nice situations, the invariants in the left column all line up. For example, as we shall see, if <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, then the cycle class map is an isomorphism and <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> is a polynomial in <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> whose coefficients are the dimensions of the even cohomology groups. However, a similar statement does not hold for <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
While reading this section, it may help to keep in mind the following simple example.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Projective space <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth and proper over <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\spec\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\spec\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The Chow and cohomology rings of <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are both generated by the first Chern class of the tautological line bundle, denoted <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta=c_{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{n}}(1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta=c_{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{n}}(1))</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, we have</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathbb{P}^{n})\cong H^{*}(\mathbb{P}^{n})=\mathbb{Q}[\zeta]/(\zeta^{n+1})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathbb{P}^{n})\cong H^{*}(\mathbb{P}^{n})=\mathbb{Q}[\zeta]/(\zeta^{n+1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">By writing <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{n}=\mathbb{A}^{n}\sqcup\mathbb{A}^{n-1}\sqcup\cdots\sqcup\mathbb{A}^{1}\sqcup\mathbb{A}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>⊔</mo><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⊔</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>⊔</mo><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>⊔</mo><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{n}=\mathbb{A}^{n}\sqcup\mathbb{A}^{n-1}\sqcup\cdots\sqcup\mathbb{A}^{1}\sqcup\mathbb{A}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>,
one also sees that</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\mathbb{P}^{n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})=q^{n}+q^{n-1}+\ldots+q+1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\mathbb{P}^{n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})=q^{n}+q^{n-1}+\ldots+q+1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">which is a polynomial in <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. Notice that the odd cohomology of <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\mathbb{P}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\mathbb{P}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes and the ranks of the even degree cohomology groups are the coefficients appearing in the polynomial above. This is an example of how the invariants line up nicely for a smooth, proper space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Now consider <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\mathbb{P}^{2}\smallsetminus E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>∖</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\mathbb{P}^{2}\smallsetminus E</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> an elliptic curve. By excision, <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is still generated by <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, since we work with rational coefficients, <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(X)=\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(X)=\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the Chow and cohomology rings are no longer isomorphic. Moreover,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#X(\mathbb{F}_{q})=\#\mathbb{P}^{2}(\mathbb{F}_{q})-\#E(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#X(\mathbb{F}_{q})=\#\mathbb{P}^{2}(\mathbb{F}_{q})-\#E(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">is not a polynomial, as <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#E(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#E(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> is not a polynomial. Although the Chow ring of <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is still relatively simple, its cohomology and point counts are more sensitive to the removal of <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>. In general, we will see that comparing the different invariants is less straightforward for spaces that are not proper.</p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Comparing Chow of <math id="S2.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The excision sequence for Chow groups says there is a right-exact sequence</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*-1}(\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\to A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\rightarrow 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*-1}(\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\to A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\rightarrow 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}\mathrel{\mathchoice{\vbox{\hbox{$\displaystyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\textstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle:$}}}{=}}\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0.278em">:</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>=</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}\mathrel{\mathchoice{\vbox{\hbox{$\displaystyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\textstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle:$}}}{=}}\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the boundary.
Thus, by Definition <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Definition 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, we immediately see the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The converse need not hold. For a concrete example, we will see in Section <a href="#S3" title="3. When are the Chow or cohomology rings tautological? ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> that
<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{2,10})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{2,10})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{2,10})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{2,10})</annotation></semantics></math> but <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,10})\neq R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,10})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,10})\neq R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,10})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Nevertheless, the excision sequence shows that if
<math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> and all classes in the image of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*-1}(\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\to A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*-1}(\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\to A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> are tautological, then
<math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the normalization of the boundary.
Since push forward along proper surjective maps is surjective on Chow groups, the
image of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*-1}(\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\to A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*-1}(\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\to A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is the same as the image of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*-1}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}})\to A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*-1}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}})\to A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the disjoint union of all possible gluing maps</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},n_{1}+1}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},n_{2}+1}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1}+g_{2},n_{1}+n_{2}}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,n+2}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},n_{1}+1}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},n_{2}+1}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1}+g_{2},n_{1}+n_{2}}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,n+2}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">At this point, to compute the Chow ring of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we need to understand the Chow ring of a product of two moduli spaces. In general, the Künneth formula does not hold for Chow rings; instead, we make the following definition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We say <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has the <em id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Chow–Künneth generation Property (CKgP)</em> if for all <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, the tensor product map</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(X)\otimes A^{*}(Y)\to A^{*}(X\times Y)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(X)\otimes A^{*}(Y)\to A^{*}(X\times Y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is surjective.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The CKgP was introduced in work of Totaro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite> and Bae–Schmitt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, and plays a key role in the author’s work with Canning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> on the Chow rings of moduli spaces of curves. The following result
illustrates what additional input may yield a converse to Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 2.2. ‣ 2.1. Comparing Chow of 
          
            
              
              
                ¯
                M
              
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  g
                  ,
                  n
                
              
            
          
         and 
          
            
              
              M
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  g
                  ,
                  n ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Lemma 4.1 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmthm4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> be given. Suppose <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP for all <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime},n^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime},n^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}\leq g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}\leq g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g^{\prime}+n^{\prime}\leq 2g+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g^{\prime}+n^{\prime}\leq 2g+n</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">The boundary of <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is surjected onto by the disjoint union of <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},n_{1}+1}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},n_{2}+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},n_{1}+1}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},n_{2}+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,n+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,n+2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each of the moduli spaces involved has either genus or number of markings smaller than <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, we can inductively assume that they all have the CKgP and tautological Chow rings. The CKgP implies that the Chow ring of <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},n_{1}+1}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},n_{2}+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},n_{1}+1}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},n_{2}+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by pullbacks of tautological classes from the two factors. Since the tautological rings are closed under pushforward along gluing maps, it follows that all classes supported on the boundary of <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> are tautological, which implies <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>. To see that <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> also has the CKgP, we use the fact that the CKgP plays well with stratifications and surjective maps, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, Lemma 4.1]</cite> for more details.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> are small, the moduli spaces <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> are unirational, and many of them can be studied very explicitly.
More precisely, in the cases where it has been shown that <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, it is because the moduli space can be stratified into pieces, each of which is surjected onto by a space whose Chow ring is not hard to calculate, such as an open substack of a Grassmann bundle over <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\BGL_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>BGL</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\BGL_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. Such tractable spaces tend to have the CKgP. Moreover, the CKgP plays well with stratifications and surjective maps, so an argument that <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> tends to also show
that <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP, cf Remark <a href="#S3.Thmthm1" title="Remark 3.1. ‣ 3. When are the Chow or cohomology rings tautological? ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. When <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> are large however, the moduli spaces <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> are of general type, so in particular do not admit such nice unirational parameterizations. While this means the techniques used for small <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> necessarily break down for large <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, we do not know of direct arguments in general for the existence of non-tautological classes or failure of CKgP just based on the birational geometry of the moduli space.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Comparing Chow and cohomology</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The cycle class map <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cl:A^{*}(\mathcal{M})\to H^{*}(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cl</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cl:A^{*}(\mathcal{M})\to H^{*}(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> is a ring homomorphism whose image lies in even degree. In general, the cycle class map need not be injective or surjective, but we have the following lemmas.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Lemma 3.11 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmthm6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth, proper Deligne–Mumford stack over <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP, then <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cl" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>cl</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cl</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP, the tensor product map <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(X)\otimes A^{*}(X)\to A^{*}(X\times X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(X)\otimes A^{*}(X)\to A^{*}(X\times X)</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective. In particular,
we obtain an algebraic decomposition of the class of the diagonal in <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times X</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies that the cycle class map is an isomorphism.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In Example <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Example 2.1. ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Somewhat surprisingly, another sufficient criterion for the cycle class map to be an isomorphism is that the Chow ring is a countable <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> vector space. This is essentially a consequence of work of Kimura and Totaro. The precise references and form stated below can be found in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, Lemma 7.1]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.7</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Lemma 7.1 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmthm7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth, proper Deligne–Mumford stack over <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is a countable <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> vector space, then <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cl" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>cl</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cl</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Because decorated boundary strata generate <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, the ring is finitely generated. Thus, we have the following corollary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2.8</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We note that the analogue of Corollary <a href="#S2.Thmthm8" title="Corollary 2.8. ‣ 2.2. Comparing Chow and cohomology ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a> is not true for <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, we have that <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{0,4})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{0,4})=\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{0,4})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{0,4})=\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> but <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(\mathcal{M}_{0,4})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(\mathcal{M}_{0,4})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{0,4})\neq RH^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{0,4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{0,4})\neq RH^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{0,4})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.9</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, it is conjectured that Pixton’s relations are complete in Chow in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, Conjecture 2]</cite>. It has also been conjectured that Pixton’s relations are complete in cohomology. This conjecture would imply in particular that
the cycle class map restricts to an isomorphism on <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\mathcal{M})\to RH^{*}(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\mathcal{M})\to RH^{*}(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math>. While this is known to hold in some cases, such as in the situation of Corollary <a href="#S2.Thmthm8" title="Corollary 2.8. ‣ 2.2. Comparing Chow and cohomology ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a>, it remains open in general.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">For the open moduli spaces <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the cycle class map is rarely an isomorphism. We write <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{i}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{i}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> for the weight <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> part of Deligne’s weight filtration. The image of the cycle class map is always contained in the lowest weight subspace <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We say that the cohomology is <em id="S2.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Tate type</em> if its semisimplification is a sum of Tate twists (see Section <a href="#S2.SS4" title="2.4. Comparing cohomology and point counts ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>).
The following lemma can be thought of as a weaker replacement of Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm7" title="Lemma 2.7 (Lemma 7.1 of []). ‣ 2.2. Comparing Chow and cohomology ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a> for spaces which are not necessarily proper.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.10</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Lemma 4.3 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmthm10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be an open substack of a smooth, proper Deligne–Mumford stack over <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has the CKgP, then</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cl:A^{*}(X)\to\bigoplus_{k}W_{k}H^{k}(X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cl</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mi>k</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cl:A^{*}(X)\to\bigoplus_{k}W_{k}H^{k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is surjective. In particular, if <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is odd, then <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(X)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(X)=0</annotation></semantics></math> and if <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is even, then <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is Tate type.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>Comparing cohomology of <math id="S2.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast with Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 2.2. ‣ 2.1. Comparing Chow of 
          
            
              
              
                ¯
                M
              
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  g
                  ,
                  n
                
              
            
          
         and 
          
            
              
              M
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  g
                  ,
                  n ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, the restriction map <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\to H^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\to H^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is usually not surjective. For example, <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,4})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,4})=0</annotation></semantics></math> but <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(\mathcal{M}_{0,4})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(\mathcal{M}_{0,4})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. The key to comparing the cohomology of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is Deligne’s weight spectral sequence. In this section, we explain how the graded pieces of the weight filtration are determined by the cohomology of the <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g^{\prime}+n^{\prime}\leq 2g+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g^{\prime}+n^{\prime}\leq 2g+n</annotation></semantics></math>, along with their <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> actions and pullback maps. For the later applications, it will be convenient to state results for compactly supported cohomology <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> and its weight filtration, but since <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth, this is dual to ordinary cohomology.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We write</p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\gr_{k}H^{*}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\mathrel{\mathchoice{\vbox{\hbox{$\displaystyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\textstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle:$}}}{=}}W_{k}H^{*}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})/W_{k-1}H_{c}^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>:</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>=</mo></mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{k}H^{*}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})\mathrel{\mathchoice{\vbox{\hbox{$\displaystyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\textstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle:$}}}{=}}W_{k}H^{*}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})/W_{k-1}H_{c}^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">for the weight <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> graded piece. We now explain how the weight <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> graded piece of cohomology is determined by the pullback maps on the <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>th cohomology of all boundary strata of <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, Section 2.3]</cite>.
This is a special case of a more general method for computing compactly supported cohomology groups of an open stratum in stratified spaces, explained in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>, which also makes precise the idea of taking the Poincaré dual of Deligne’s weight spectral sequence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> be a dual graph and let <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> be the product of moduli spaces as in (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>). Then <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aut\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aut\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})</annotation></semantics></math> by permuting the point in a way corresponding to the action of <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aut\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aut\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> on half-edges. We also require the representation <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det E(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det E(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>, where each element of <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aut\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aut\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> acts by the sign of the corresponding permutation of the edges of <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. The Poincaré dual of Deligne’s weight spectral sequence gives rise to a complex</p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\xrightarrow{d_{0}}\bigoplus_{|E(\Gamma)|=1}H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}\otimes\det E(\Gamma))^{\Aut\Gamma}\xrightarrow{d_{1}}\bigoplus_{|E(\Gamma)|=2}H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}\otimes\det E(\Gamma))^{\Aut\Gamma}\xrightarrow{d_{2}}\dots." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.111em">→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">0</mn></msub></mover><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.055em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.111em">→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub></mover><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.055em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msub></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\xrightarrow{d_{0}}\bigoplus_{|E(\Gamma)|=1}H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}\otimes\det E(\Gamma))^{\Aut\Gamma}\xrightarrow{d_{1}}\bigoplus_{|E(\Gamma)|=2}H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}\otimes\det E(\Gamma))^{\Aut\Gamma}\xrightarrow{d_{2}}\dots.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the pullback to the normalization of the boundary.
When there is an edge contraction <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\to\Gamma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\to\Gamma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, the factor of <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> between those corresponding components is given by the associated pullback, with an appropriate sign.
The <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th homology of this complex computes</p>
<table id="S2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{k}H_{c}^{j+k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=\frac{\ker d_{j}}{\im d_{j-1}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{k}H_{c}^{j+k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=\frac{\ker d_{j}}{\im d_{j-1}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4. </span>Comparing cohomology and point counts</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a Deligne–Mumford stack <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of finite type over <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, one defines its number of points over <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\#X(\mathbb{F}_{q})\mathrel{\mathchoice{\vbox{\hbox{$\displaystyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\textstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle:$}}}{=}}\sum_{\xi\in[X(\mathbb{F}_{q})]}\frac{1}{|\Aut(\xi)|}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>:</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo></mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#X(\mathbb{F}_{q})\mathrel{\mathchoice{\vbox{\hbox{$\displaystyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\textstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle:$}}}{=}}\sum_{\xi\in[X(\mathbb{F}_{q})]}\frac{1}{|\Aut(\xi)|},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the sum ranges over representatives of isomorphism classes of objects in <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math>. This “stacky” point count agrees with the regular point count of the coarse moduli space of <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The essential tool relating point counts and cohomology is the Grothendieck–Lefschetz trace formula.
The inspiration and name comes from the classical Lefschetz fixed point theorem in topology, which computes the size of the fixed locus of an endomorphism in terms of the trace of its pullback acting on cohomology. If <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:X\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:X\to X</annotation></semantics></math> is an endomorphism, then the fixed locus of <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is the intersection of the diagonal <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\subset X\times X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\subset X\times X</annotation></semantics></math> with the graph <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\phi}\subset X\times X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\phi}\subset X\times X</annotation></semantics></math>. We say that the fixed locus is <em id="S2.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">simple</em> if the intersection of <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> is transverse, equivalently if the fixed locus consists of reduced, isolated fixed points.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.11</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Lefschetz fixed point theorem)<span id="S2.Thmthm11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact, oriented manifold. If <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:X\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:X\to X</annotation></semantics></math> is an endomorphism with simple fixed locus, then</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\text{(fixed points of $\phi$)}=\sum_{i}(-1)^{i}\tr(\phi^{*}|H^{i}(X))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">(fixed points of </mtext><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">)</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\text{(fixed points of $\phi$)}=\sum_{i}(-1)^{i}\tr(\phi^{*}|H^{i}(X)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To explain the connection with point counts over finite fields, recall that given a variety <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>-points of <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> are precisely the fixed locus of the Frobenius endomorphism. Although <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is not longer a compact, oriented manifold, a similar formula for the size of the fixed locus holds in an appropriate cohomology theory. We write <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}_{c,\mathrm{\acute{e}t}}(-,\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>´</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}_{c,\mathrm{\acute{e}t}}(-,\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> for compactly supported <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-adic cohomology. We refer the reader to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> for background on étale cohomology.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.12</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Behrend–Grothendiek–Lefschetz trace formula <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmthm12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth Deligne–Mumford stack over <math id="S2.Thmthm12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\spec\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\spec\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S2.Thmthm12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> be a prime with <math id="S2.Thmthm12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\ell,q)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\ell,q)=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#X(\mathbb{F}_{q})=\sum_{i}(-1)^{i}\tr(\mathrm{Frob}_{q}^{*}|H^{i}_{c,\mathrm{\acute{e}t}}(X_{\overline{\mathbb{F}}_{q}},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell}))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Frob</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>´</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#X(\mathbb{F}_{q})=\sum_{i}(-1)^{i}\tr(\mathrm{Frob}_{q}^{*}|H^{i}_{c,\mathrm{\acute{e}t}}(X_{\overline{\mathbb{F}}_{q}},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The compactly supported étale cohomology groups also admit a weight filtration.
Work of Deligne <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> states that the eigenvalues of <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Frob}_{q}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Frob</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Frob}_{q}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> acting on the weight <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> graded piece are all Weil numbers of of weight <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that under any embedding <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathbb{Q}_{\ell}}\hookrightarrow\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathbb{Q}_{\ell}}\hookrightarrow\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>,
they are algebraic integers of absolute value <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{k/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{k/2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, the characteristic polynomial of <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Frob}_{q}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Frob</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Frob}_{q}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of the choice of the auxiliary prime <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> that goes into the construction of <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-adic étale cohomology.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 2.13</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let us count <math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>-points on <math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> using the trace formula. The endomorphism <math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Frob}_{q}:\mathbb{P}^{1}\to\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Frob</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Frob}_{q}:\mathbb{P}^{1}\to\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by <math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[x:y]\mapsto[x^{q}:y^{q}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[x:y]\mapsto[x^{q}:y^{q}]</annotation></semantics></math>.
We have <math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}_{c,\mathrm{\acute{e}t}}(\mathbb{P}^{1}_{\overline{\mathbb{F}}_{q}},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>´</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}_{c,\mathrm{\acute{e}t}}(\mathbb{P}^{1}_{\overline{\mathbb{F}}_{q}},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> is spanned by the fundamental class of <math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which pulls back to the fundamental class of <math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Frob}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Frob</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Frob}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Meanwhile, <math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}_{c,\mathrm{\acute{e}t}}(\mathbb{P}^{1}_{\overline{\mathbb{F}}_{q}},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>´</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}_{c,\mathrm{\acute{e}t}}(\mathbb{P}^{1}_{\overline{\mathbb{F}}_{q}},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}_{c,\mathrm{\acute{e}t}}(\mathbb{P}^{1}_{\overline{\mathbb{F}}_{q}},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>´</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}_{c,\mathrm{\acute{e}t}}(\mathbb{P}^{1}_{\overline{\mathbb{F}}_{q}},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> is spanned by the class of a point.
Since <math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Frob}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Frob</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Frob}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is degree <math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, the pullback of a point is <math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> times the class of a point. Thus</p>
<table id="S2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{F}_{q})=\sum_{i}(-1)^{i}\tr(\mathrm{Frob}_{q}^{*}|H^{i}_{c,\mathrm{\acute{e}t}}(\mathbb{P}^{1}_{\overline{\mathbb{F}}_{q}},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell}))=1-0+q=1+q." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Frob</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>´</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{F}_{q})=\sum_{i}(-1)^{i}\tr(\mathrm{Frob}_{q}^{*}|H^{i}_{c,\mathrm{\acute{e}t}}(\mathbb{P}^{1}_{\overline{\mathbb{F}}_{q}},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell}))=1-0+q=1+q.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In what follows, we write <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathsf{L}\mathrel{\mathchoice{\vbox{\hbox{$\displaystyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\textstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle:$}}}{=}}H^{2}_{c,\mathrm{\acute{e}t}}(\mathbb{P}^{1},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖫</mi><mrow><mo lspace="0.278em">:</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>=</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>´</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}\mathrel{\mathchoice{\vbox{\hbox{$\displaystyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\textstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle:$}}}{=}}H^{2}_{c,\mathrm{\acute{e}t}}(\mathbb{P}^{1},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathsf{L}^{i}\mathrel{\mathchoice{\vbox{\hbox{$\displaystyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\textstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle:$}}}{=}}\mathsf{L}^{\otimes i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖫</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mrow><mo lspace="0.278em">:</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>=</mo></mrow><mi>𝖫</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}^{i}\mathrel{\mathchoice{\vbox{\hbox{$\displaystyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\textstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle:$}}}{=}}\mathsf{L}^{\otimes i}</annotation></semantics></math>, called the <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th Tate twist (often also denoted <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}_{\ell}(-i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}_{\ell}(-i)</annotation></semantics></math>). The <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th Tate twist is a <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional Galois representation on which <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Frob}_{q}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Frob</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Frob}_{q}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> acts by <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If the semi-simplification of <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}_{c,\mathrm{\acute{e}t}}(X,\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>´</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}_{c,\mathrm{\acute{e}t}}(X,\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> is a sum of Tate twists, we say the cohomology of <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Tate type</em>.
Generalizing the above example, the following lemma is an immediate consequence of the trace formula.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.14</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth Deligne–Mumford stack over <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\spec\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\spec\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If the cohomology of <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is Tate type, then <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#X(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#X(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> is a polynomial in <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">As we saw in the example with the class of a point on <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>,
algebraic cycles on <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> defined over <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> always give classes of Tate type. In particular, we have the following corollary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2.15</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proposition 3.15 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmthm15.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S2.Thmthm15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S2.Thmthm15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> is a polynomial in <math id="S2.Thmthm15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Less obvious is that the converse of Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm14" title="Lemma 2.14. ‣ 2.4. Comparing cohomology and point counts ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.14</span></a> holds when <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is also assumed to be proper. In fact, having polynomial point count determines the cohomology of <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> as a Galois representation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.16</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(van den Bogaart–Edixhoven <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmthm16.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth, proper Deligne–Mumford stack over <math id="S2.Thmthm16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\spec\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\spec\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S2.Thmthm16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#X(\mathbb{F}_{q})=a_{d}q^{d}+\cdots+a_{1}q+a_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#X(\mathbb{F}_{q})=a_{d}q^{d}+\cdots+a_{1}q+a_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a polynomial in <math id="S2.Thmthm16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> , then all odd cohomology groups of <math id="S2.Thmthm16.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> vanish and the even cohomology groups are pure Tate</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2i}_{\mathrm{\acute{e}t}}(X,\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})=(\mathsf{L}^{i})^{\oplus a_{i}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>´</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2i}_{\mathrm{\acute{e}t}}(X,\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})=(\mathsf{L}^{i})^{\oplus a_{i}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.17</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm17.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As we shall see later on, in every case where <math id="S2.Thmthm17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> is known to be a polynomial in <math id="S2.Thmthm17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, it is also known that <math id="S2.Thmthm17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm16" title="Theorem 2.16 (van den Bogaart–Edixhoven []). ‣ 2.4. Comparing cohomology and point counts ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.16</span></a> says that if <math id="S2.Thmthm17.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> is a polynomial in <math id="S2.Thmthm17.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, then the cohomology of <math id="S2.Thmthm17.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is pure Tate. The Tate conjecture then predicts that the cycle class map is surjective. However, the statement that <math id="S2.Thmthm17.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is stronger than the claim that the cycle class map is surjective.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">One of the key ways properness is used in Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm16" title="Theorem 2.16 (van den Bogaart–Edixhoven []). ‣ 2.4. Comparing cohomology and point counts ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.16</span></a> is that, when <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is proper, weight <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> eigenvalues only occur in <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is not proper, the converse to Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm14" title="Lemma 2.14. ‣ 2.4. Comparing cohomology and point counts ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.14</span></a> need not hold because, a priori, some non-Tate eigenvalues could occur equally often in odd and even cohomological degrees, and cancel each other in the alternating sum (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In Theorem 2.12 (Behrend–Grothendiek–Lefschetz trace formula []). ‣ 2.4. Comparing cohomology and point counts ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>).
Motivated by the possibility of such cancellation, we define the <em id="S2.SS4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">weight <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> Euler characteristic of <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math></em> to be</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{k}(X)=\sum_{i}(-1)^{i}\dim\gr_{k}H^{i}_{c}(X)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{k}(X)=\sum_{i}(-1)^{i}\dim\gr_{k}H^{i}_{c}(X).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p8.2" class="ltx_p">With this notation, we have the following result, even if <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is not proper. (If <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is proper, then the result follows immediately from the contrapositive of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm16" title="Theorem 2.16 (van den Bogaart–Edixhoven []). ‣ 2.4. Comparing cohomology and point counts ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.16</span></a>.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm18" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.18</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm18.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proposition 2.3 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmthm18.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm18.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm18.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm18.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth Deligne–Mumford stack over <math id="S2.Thmthm18.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\spec\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\spec\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.Thmthm18.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#X(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#X(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> is a polynomial in <math id="S2.Thmthm18.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.Thmthm18.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{k}(X)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{k}(X)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all odd <math id="S2.Thmthm18.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm19" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 2.19</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm19.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm19.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The first example of an interesting odd cohomology group of moduli spaces of curves is</p>
<table id="S2.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})\otimes\mathbb{C}=H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})\oplus H^{0,11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})\otimes\mathbb{C}=H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})\oplus H^{0,11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm19.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Let</p>
<table id="S2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(q)=q\prod_{m=1}^{\infty}(1-q^{m})^{24}=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\tau(n)q^{n}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>24</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(q)=q\prod_{m=1}^{\infty}(1-q^{m})^{24}=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\tau(n)q^{n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm19.p1.3" class="ltx_p">be the weight <math id="S2.Thmthm19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> cusp form for <math id="S2.Thmthm19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SL_{2}\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>SL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SL_{2}\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. (We apologize for the clash of notation; in this one instance above, <math id="S2.Thmthm19.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=e^{2\pi iz}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=e^{2\pi iz}</annotation></semantics></math> for a complex variable <math id="S2.Thmthm19.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>.)
From the weight <math id="S2.Thmthm19.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> cusp form, one can construct an explicit holomorphic form on <math id="S2.Thmthm19.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}</annotation></semantics></math>, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, p. 306]</cite>.
Meanwhile, having odd weight, the Galois representation <math id="S2.Thmthm19.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathrm{\acute{e}t}}^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>´</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>11</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathrm{\acute{e}t}}^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> is not Tate type. In fact, it is a <math id="S2.Thmthm19.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional irreducible Galois representation which satisfies</p>
<table id="S2.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tr(\mathrm{Frob}_{p}^{*}|H^{11}_{\mathrm{\acute{e}t}}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell}))=\tau(p)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Frob</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>´</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>11</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tr(\mathrm{Frob}_{p}^{*}|H^{11}_{\mathrm{\acute{e}t}}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell}))=\tau(p).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm19.p1.4" class="ltx_p">It turns out that all other odd cohomology groups of <math id="S2.Thmthm19.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}</annotation></semantics></math> vanish and the even cohomology groups are Tate type.
Consequently, the point counts of <math id="S2.Thmthm19.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}</annotation></semantics></math> have the form</p>
<table id="S2.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}(\mathbb{F}_{p})=P(p)-\tau(p)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}(\mathbb{F}_{p})=P(p)-\tau(p)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm19.p1.5" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.Thmthm19.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(p)</annotation></semantics></math> is a polynomial in <math id="S2.Thmthm19.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, Section 2.2]</cite> for more details.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">More generally, for each Hecke eigenform <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> of weight <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SL_{2}\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>SL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SL_{2}\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, there is an associated <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional irreducible Galois representation of weight <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math> on which <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Frob}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Frob</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Frob}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> acts by the <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>th Fourier coefficient of <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. Following the notation of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, for the eigenforms of weights <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=12,16,18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=12,16,18</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote this associated Galois representation by <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
There are also irreducible Galois representations, denoted <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{j,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{j,k}</annotation></semantics></math> of weight <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j+2k-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j+2k-3</annotation></semantics></math>, which come from genus <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> and
are associated to Siegel cusp forms for <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Sp}_{4}(\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Sp</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Sp}_{4}(\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">This association is part of a larger web of conjectures in the Langlands program which says roughly that there should be a bijection between algebraic automorphic representations and irreducible geometric Galois representations. A result of
Chenevier and Lannes classifies all cuspidal automorphic representations of conductor <math id="S2.SS4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and weight up to <math id="S2.SS4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>22</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">22</annotation></semantics></math>. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, Bergström and Faber explain how, in combination with this classification result, these Langlands conjectures would then restrict the Galois representations of weight at most <math id="S2.SS4.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>22</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">22</annotation></semantics></math> that appear in the cohomology of Deligne–Mumford stacks that are smooth and proper over <math id="S2.SS4.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\spec\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\spec\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm20" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conj">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 2.20</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm20.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm20.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm20.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm20.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm20.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth, proper Delgne–Mumford stack over <math id="S2.Thmthm20.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\spec\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\spec\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, such as <math id="S2.Thmthm20.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose <math id="S2.Thmthm20.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>22</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 22</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the semi-simplification
<math id="S2.Thmthm20.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(X,\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})^{\mathrm{ss}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(X,\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})^{\mathrm{ss}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sum of Tate twists of the following Galois representations</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1},\ \mathsf{S}_{12},\ \mathsf{S}_{16},\ \mathsf{S}_{18},\ \mathsf{S}_{20},\mathsf{S}_{6,8},\ \mathsf{S}_{22},\ \mathsf{S}_{4,10},\ \mathsf{S}_{8,8},\ \mathsf{S}_{12,6},\ \Sym^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>16</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>18</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>20</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1},\ \mathsf{S}_{12},\ \mathsf{S}_{16},\ \mathsf{S}_{18},\ \mathsf{S}_{20},\mathsf{S}_{6,8},\ \mathsf{S}_{22},\ \mathsf{S}_{4,10},\ \mathsf{S}_{8,8},\ \mathsf{S}_{12,6},\ \Sym^{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p11.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.SS4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> a smooth and proper Delgne–Mumford stack of dimension <math id="S2.SS4.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S2.SS4.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(X,\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(X,\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> is pure weight <math id="S2.SS4.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning the eigenvalues of <math id="S2.SS4.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Frob}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Frob</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Frob}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> acting on <math id="S2.SS4.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(X,\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(X,\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> all have absolute value <math id="S2.SS4.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{k/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{k/2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the leading order terms in the point counts <math id="S2.SS4.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#X(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#X(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> come from the highest degree cohomology groups.
Poincaré duality shows that, as Galois representations, the high degree cohomology groups differ from corresponding low degree cohomology groups by a Tate twist: <math id="S2.SS4.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2d-k}(X,\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})\cong\mathsf{L}^{d-k}\otimes H^{k}(X,\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2d-k}(X,\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})\cong\mathsf{L}^{d-k}\otimes H^{k}(X,\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, using the trace formula, Conjecture <a href="#S2.Thmthm20" title="Conjecture 2.20. ‣ 2.4. Comparing cohomology and point counts ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.20</span></a> implies loosely speaking that <math id="S2.SS4.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#X(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#X(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> is well-approximated by a polynomial in <math id="S2.SS4.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, with the leading order correction terms coming from coefficients of moduli forms and Siegel modular forms.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.5. </span>Comparing point counts of <math id="S2.SS5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The point counts of <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> are related by the equation</p>
<table id="S2.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})=\#\mathcal{M}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})+\#\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})=\#\mathcal{M}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})+\#\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed by summing up point counts for each locally closed boundary stratum. Each boundary stratum is a finite group quotient of a product of <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g^{\prime}+n^{\prime}\leq 2g+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g^{\prime}+n^{\prime}\leq 2g+n</annotation></semantics></math>. The point count of a product is the product of the point counts, so one might hope to translate between the following two sets of information</p>
<table id="S2.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\#\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}(\mathbb{F}_{q}):2g^{\prime}+n^{\prime}\leq c\}\overset{?}{\longleftrightarrow}\{\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}(\mathbb{F}_{q}):2g^{\prime}+n^{\prime}\leq c\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">⟷</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">?</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\#\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}(\mathbb{F}_{q}):2g^{\prime}+n^{\prime}\leq c\}\overset{?}{\longleftrightarrow}\{\#\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}(\mathbb{F}_{q}):2g^{\prime}+n^{\prime}\leq c\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">However, some additional care is needed because point counts do not always play well with quotients by finite groups. Instead, we shall see that the sets of information above become equivalent when we pass to their equivariant versions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The reason we need equivariant point counts is because, although it holds for <em id="S2.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">connected</em> linear algebraic groups, the naive equality <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#[X/G](\mathbb{F}_{q})=\#X(\mathbb{F}_{q})/\#G(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>#</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#[X/G](\mathbb{F}_{q})=\#X(\mathbb{F}_{q})/\#G(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> need not hold for finite groups <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, consider <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=\mathbb{Z}/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=\mathbb{Z}/2</annotation></semantics></math> acting on <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the non-trivial element acts by <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\mapsto-t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0em" stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\mapsto-t</annotation></semantics></math>. The quotient stack <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathbb{A}^{1}/G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathbb{A}^{1}/G]</annotation></semantics></math> has coarse moduli space <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, we find</p>
<table id="S2.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\mathbb{A}^{1}(\mathbb{F}_{q})=q\qquad\text{and}\qquad\#[\mathbb{A}^{1}/G](\mathbb{F}_{q})=\#\mathbb{A}^{1}(\mathbb{F}_{q})=q." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\mathbb{A}^{1}(\mathbb{F}_{q})=q\qquad\text{and}\qquad\#[\mathbb{A}^{1}/G](\mathbb{F}_{q})=\#\mathbb{A}^{1}(\mathbb{F}_{q})=q.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The resolution to this seemly strange statement is that an <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>-point of the quotient could come either from two <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> points on <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> that form a <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> orbit, or from two conjugate <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> points whose orbit is still defined over <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In general, equivariant point counts are defined using <em id="S2.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">twisted forms</em>.
Given a variety <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in\Aut(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in\Aut(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a unique twisted form of <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>σ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> with an isomorphism <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\sigma}_{\overline{\mathbb{F}}_{q}}\to X_{\overline{\mathbb{F}}_{q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><mi>σ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\sigma}_{\overline{\mathbb{F}}_{q}}\to X_{\overline{\mathbb{F}}_{q}}</annotation></semantics></math> that identifies the geometric Frobenius action on <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\sigma}_{\overline{\mathbb{F}}_{q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><mi>σ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\sigma}_{\overline{\mathbb{F}}_{q}}</annotation></semantics></math> with the composition of <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and the Frobenius on <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\overline{\mathbb{F}}_{q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\overline{\mathbb{F}}_{q}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in G=\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in G=\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and some fixed <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>,
there is a twisted form <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>σ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a smooth Deligne–Mumford stack over <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> representing the functor taking a scheme <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> to the set of isomorphism classes of families of (stable) curves over <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> disjoint labeled geometric sections such that Frobenius acts on the sections via the permutation <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given a finite group <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> acting on <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, conjugate elements of <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> induce isomorphic twisted forms of <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#X^{\sigma}(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>σ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#X^{\sigma}(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> is a class function on <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. The <em id="S2.SS5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S2.SS5.p3.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant point count</em>, denoted <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#^{G}X(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>#</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#^{G}X(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math>, is the element of the representation ring of <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> associated to the class function <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\mapsto\#X^{\sigma}(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>σ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\mapsto\#X^{\sigma}(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math>.
By the Grothendeick–Lefschetz trace formula, <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#X(\mathbb{F}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#X(\mathbb{F}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by the virtual Galois representation <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m32" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\chi_{c}(X,\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})\mathrel{\mathchoice{\vbox{\hbox{$\displaystyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\textstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle:$}}}{=}}\sum(-1)^{i}H_{c}^{i}(X,\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>:</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{c}(X,\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})\mathrel{\mathchoice{\vbox{\hbox{$\displaystyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\textstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle:$}}}{=}}\sum(-1)^{i}H_{c}^{i}(X,\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, knowing the <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant point counts amount to knowing <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{c}(X,\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{c}(X,\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> as a <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant Galois representation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The Getzler–Kapranov formula <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> gives a recipe for computing one of the sets of equivariant point counts below from the other:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\#^{\mathbb{S}_{n^{\prime}}}\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}(\mathbb{F}_{q}):2g^{\prime}+n^{\prime}\leq c\}\longleftrightarrow\{\#^{\mathbb{S}_{n^{\prime}}}\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}(\mathbb{F}_{q}):2g^{\prime}+n^{\prime}\leq c\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>#</mi><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟷</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>#</mi><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\#^{\mathbb{S}_{n^{\prime}}}\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}(\mathbb{F}_{q}):2g^{\prime}+n^{\prime}\leq c\}\longleftrightarrow\{\#^{\mathbb{S}_{n^{\prime}}}\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}}(\mathbb{F}_{q}):2g^{\prime}+n^{\prime}\leq c\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The Getzler–Kapranov formula is essentially a combinatorial version of the spectral sequence discussed in Section <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3. Comparing cohomology of 
          
            
              
              
                ¯
                M
              
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  g
                  ,
                  n
                
              
            
          
         and 
          
            
              
              M
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  g
                  ,
                  n ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>When are the Chow or cohomology rings tautological?</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we survey some results on the Chow and cohomology rings of <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, with particular attention to when the ring is generated by tautological classes or not. We start with the case of the Chow ring, summarizing the results with the chart below.
We then note where some stronger results are known in cohomology.
In the chart below, we summarize the known results by placing a symbol from the key at position <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math> when the corresponding condition is known to hold for <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
