<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The implications next to the symbol key are from Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 2.2. ‣ 2.1. Comparing Chow of 
          
            
              
              
                ¯
                M
              
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  g
                  ,
                  n
                
              
            
          
         and 
          
            
              
              M
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  g
                  ,
                  n ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> and its contrapositive.
When the chart is completed, every position will be filled with exactly one of the following symbols</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.pic3" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="23.98" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 269.66 23.98" width="269.66"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,23.98) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(4.7,0) translate(0,11.99)"><path d="M 0 0 M 4.43 0 C 4.43 2.45 2.45 4.43 0 4.43 C -2.45 4.43 -4.43 2.45 -4.43 0 C -4.43 -2.45 -2.45 -4.43 0 -4.43 C 2.45 -4.43 4.43 -2.45 4.43 0 Z M 0 0"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FF0000;--ltx-fill-color:#FF0000;--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" color="#FF0000" fill="#FF0000" stroke="#FF0000" transform="matrix(0.75 0.0 0.0 0.75 121.95 -2.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-fo-width:0.78em;--ltx-fo-height:0.58em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.08em;font-size:10pt;" color="#000000" height="9.22" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.07)" width="10.76"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.pic3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\times}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathcolor="#FF0000" mathvariant="bold">×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\times}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g stroke-width="1.6pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 125.98 0 M 130.41 0 C 130.41 2.45 128.43 4.43 125.98 4.43 C 123.54 4.43 121.56 2.45 121.56 0 C 121.56 -2.45 123.54 -4.43 125.98 -4.43 C 128.43 -4.43 130.41 -2.45 130.41 0 Z M 125.98 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FF0000;--ltx-fill-color:#FF0000;--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" color="#FF0000" fill="#FF0000" stroke="#FF0000" transform="matrix(1.3 0.0 0.0 1.3 244.97 -4.5)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-fo-width:0.78em;--ltx-fo-height:0.58em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.08em;font-size:10pt;" color="#000000" height="9.22" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.07)" width="10.76"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.pic3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\times}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathcolor="#FF0000" mathvariant="bold">×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\times}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Currently, in every case where a filled circle appears in the chart, <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is known to have the CKgP. Similarly, in every instance where an open circle appears in the chart, <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is known to have the CKgP.</p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Genus 0</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In genus <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{0,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{0,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is an open subset of affine space, so the Chow ring is trivial <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{0,n})=\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{0,n})=\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows quickly from excision and the structure of the boundary that the Chow groups
<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n})</annotation></semantics></math> are generated by fundamental classes of boundary strata and are hence tautological. The relations among boundary strata and the ring structure of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is computed in work of Keel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Genus 1</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The fact that <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 10</annotation></semantics></math> was established in the thesis of Belorousski <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. Meanwhile, it is known that <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. This means that <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})\neq RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})\neq RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math> and so by Corollary <a href="#S2.Thmthm8" title="Corollary 2.8. ‣ 2.2. Comparing Chow and cohomology ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a>, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})\neq R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})\neq R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, the presence of odd cohomology means that the cycle class map cannot be an isomorphism, so by Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm7" title="Lemma 2.7 (Lemma 7.1 of []). ‣ 2.2. Comparing Chow and cohomology ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>, we conclude that <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math> is an uncountably infinite dimensional vector space over <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Going further, it is known that <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, p. 306]</cite> for an explicit construction of a holomorphic form.
On a smooth, proper variety <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, the presence of a holomorphic <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> form for <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> implies that the Chow group of zero cycles is so large it cannot even be dominated by a finite-dimensional variety.
References and an explanation are given in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, Remark 1.1]</cite> for why the same conclusion holds for the smooth, proper Deligne–Mumford stack <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Because the push forwards along forgetful maps are surjective, the fact that
<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math> is infinite-dimensional implies that <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is infinite-dimensional for all <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 11</annotation></semantics></math>.
Some additional care is needed to argue that the Chow rings of the open moduli spaces are also infinite-dimensional, thus giving the large red exes in the rest of the genus <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> column.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 11</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is an infinite-dimensional vector space. In particular, <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})\neq R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})\neq R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">We first prove the claim for <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=11</annotation></semantics></math>. We know that <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math> is infinite-dimensional, so by excision, it suffices to show that <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\partial\mathcal{M}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\partial\mathcal{M}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math> is finite-dimensional. The boundary
of <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}</annotation></semantics></math> is the image of gluing maps from products of genus <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> moduli spaces and genus <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> moduli spaces with at most <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> markings. All genus <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> moduli spaces have the CKgP, we have the following sequence of surjective maps</span></p><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="142.96" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-71.48px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 320 142.96" width="320"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,142.96) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(160,0) translate(0,71.48)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -160 -62.46)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 142.96)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 15.44)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(160,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -154.04 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:22.27em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:1em;font-size:10pt;" height="24.22" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="308.09"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,13})\oplus\bigoplus_{A\subset\{1,\ldots,11\}}A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A\cup p})\otimes A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,A^{c}\cup p^{\prime}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,13})\oplus\bigoplus_{A\subset\{1,\ldots,11\}}A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A\cup p})\otimes A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,A^{c}\cup p^{\prime}})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 74.69)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 16.83 0) translate(143.18,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -137.22 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:19.83em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:1em;font-size:10pt;" height="24.22" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="274.44"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,13})\oplus\bigoplus_{A\subset\{1,\ldots,11\}}A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A\cup p}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,A^{c}\cup p^{\prime}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,13})\oplus\bigoplus_{A\subset\{1,\ldots,11\}}A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A\cup p}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,A^{c}\cup p^{\prime}})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 133.94)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 118.17 0) translate(41.83,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -35.87 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.19em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.34" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="71.75"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A^{*}(\partial\mathcal{M}_{1,11}).}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A^{*}(\partial\mathcal{M}_{1,11}).}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 36.86 L 0 13.06"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 0 12.79)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 -22.39 L 0 -46.19"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 0 -46.46)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g></g></svg></span>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">In the sum in the top row, we sum over <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A^{c}|\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A^{c}|\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> so that the genus <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> curve that is glued to the genus <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> curve is stable.
Thus, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A\cup p|\leq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A\cup p|\leq 10</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A\cup p})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A\cup p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A\cup p})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A\cup p})</annotation></semantics></math> is finite-dimensional. Hence, the
vector space in the top row above is finite-dimensional. It follows that <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\partial\mathcal{M}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\partial\mathcal{M}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math> is finite-dimensional, which completes the claim for <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=11</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now assume for contradiction that the lemma is false. Then, there exists some <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 11</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is an infinite-dimensional vector space but <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n+1})</annotation></semantics></math> is finite-dimensional.
Because <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is infinite-dimensional, so is <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> be the minimal index such that <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{i}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{i}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is infinite-dimensional. Because <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n+1}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n+1}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n}</annotation></semantics></math> has a section, the pullback map is injective. Hence, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{i}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{i}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n+1})</annotation></semantics></math> is infinite-dimensional. By excision, it suffices to show that <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{i-1}(\partial\mathcal{M}_{1,n+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{i-1}(\partial\mathcal{M}_{1,n+1})</annotation></semantics></math> is finite-dimensional, as this will contradict the assumption that <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{1,n+1})</annotation></semantics></math> is finite-dimensional.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Because all <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,m}</annotation></semantics></math> have the CKgP, we have a sequence of surjective maps</span></p><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS2.p5.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="146.44" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-73.22px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 404.52 146.44" width="404.52"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,146.44) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(202.26,0) translate(0,73.22)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -202.26 -64.2)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 146.44)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 16.81)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(202.26,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -196.3 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:28.37em;--ltx-fo-height:0.85em;--ltx-fo-depth:1em;font-size:10pt;" height="25.59" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.75)" width="392.6"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p5.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A^{i-1}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n+3})\oplus\bigoplus_{A\subset\{1,\ldots,n+1\}}\bigoplus_{j+k=i-1}A^{j}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A\cup p})\otimes A^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,A^{c}\cup p^{\prime}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A^{i-1}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n+3})\oplus\bigoplus_{A\subset\{1,\ldots,n+1\}}\bigoplus_{j+k=i-1}A^{j}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A\cup p})\otimes A^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,A^{c}\cup p^{\prime}})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 77.11)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 41.77 0) translate(160.49,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -154.53 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:22.34em;--ltx-fo-height:0.83em;--ltx-fo-depth:1em;font-size:10pt;" height="25.27" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.43)" width="309.06"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p5.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A^{i-1}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n+3})\oplus\bigoplus_{A\subset\{1,\ldots,n+1\}}A^{i-1}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A\cup p}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,A^{c}\cup p^{\prime}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A^{i-1}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n+3})\oplus\bigoplus_{A\subset\{1,\ldots,n+1\}}A^{i-1}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A\cup p}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,A^{c}\cup p^{\prime}})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 137.41)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 151.77 0) translate(50.49,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -44.53 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:6.44em;--ltx-fo-height:0.83em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.39" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.43)" width="89.06"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p5.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A^{i-1}(\partial\mathcal{M}_{1,n+1}).}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A^{i-1}(\partial\mathcal{M}_{1,n+1}).}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 37.23 L 0 13.43"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 0 13.15)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 -23.07 L 0 -46.87"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 0 -47.15)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g></g></svg></span>
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">Here, the sum runs over <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A^{c}|\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A^{c}|\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A\cup p|\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A\cup p|\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>.
By the minimality of <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, we know that <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{j}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{j}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is finite-dimensional for all <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\leq i-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\leq i-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Because pullbacks along the forgetful maps are injective, it follows that <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{j}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A\cup p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{j}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A\cup p})</annotation></semantics></math> is finite dimensional for all <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A\cup p|\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A\cup p|\leq n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\leq i-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\leq i-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, the vector space at the top of the previous display is finite-dimensional. We conclude that <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{i-1}(\partial\mathcal{M}_{1,n+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{i-1}(\partial\mathcal{M}_{1,n+1})</annotation></semantics></math> is finite-dimensional, as desired.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Since the Chow groups are so large, there are plenty of non-tautological classes in <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Nevertheless, the following result implies that their images under the cycle class map are all tautological in cohomology.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Petersen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmthm4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})=RH^{2k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})=RH^{2k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is spanned by fundamental classes of boundary strata.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Tautological classes in genus 2 through 9</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The filled and open circles in genus <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> through <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math> were established <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> with the following exceptions (some of the open circles referenced below were established earlier, and are then covered in the chart with a filled circle from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>):</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> by Mumford <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math> by Faber <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> by Faber <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S3.I1.ix1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldmath{\circ}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∘</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldmath{\circ}}</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> by Faber <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S3.I1.ix2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldmath{\circ}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∘</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldmath{\circ}}</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> by Izadi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S3.I1.ix3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldmath{\circ}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∘</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldmath{\circ}}</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I1.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=8,9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=8,9</annotation></semantics></math> by Liu <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.ix4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S3.I1.ix4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldmath{\circ}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∘</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldmath{\circ}}</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.ix4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=6</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I1.ix4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> by Penev–Vakil <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.ix5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S3.I1.ix5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldmath{\circ}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∘</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldmath{\circ}}</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.ix5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=7</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I1.ix5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq n\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq n\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math> by Canning–Larson–Payne <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.ix6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S3.I1.ix6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldmath{\circ}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∘</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldmath{\circ}}</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.ix6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=7,8,9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=7,8,9</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I1.ix6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> by Canning–Larson <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Each of these works involves explicitly constructing stratifications of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and then determining the Chow rings of each stratum by using convenient models of the pointed curve. For example, in genus <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, we stratify <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{3,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{3,n}</annotation></semantics></math> into the hyperelliptic and non-hyperelliptic curves. The non-hyperelliptic curves are smooth plane quartics. It turns out that if <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 11</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> points lying on a smooth plane quartic always impose independent conditions on smooth plane quartics. In this way, the non-hyperelliptic locus of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{3,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{3,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is realized as an open substack of a vector bundle over an open substack of the moduli space of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> distinct points on <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. This sort of description furnishes generators for the Chow ring of the stratum. Finally, one checks that these generators are tautological.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The case <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=8,9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=8,9</annotation></semantics></math> is particularly interesting. As above, one first separates out the curves of gonality <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, the tetragonal curves can be modeled as the complete intersection of three quadrics in <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 7</annotation></semantics></math>, then in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, it was shown that any <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> points on a canonical genus <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> curve impose independent conditions on quadrics in <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, this breaks down when <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 8</annotation></semantics></math>. The innovation of Liu in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> is to prove that when <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=8,9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=8,9</annotation></semantics></math> the points fail to impose independent conditions on quadrics if and only if <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> of them lie in a hyperplane. Liu then stratifies the tetragonal locus according to whether or not the <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> points impose independent conditions on quadrics, realizing each piece as an open substack of a vector bundle over a better understood moduli space of points.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Once the Chow ring of <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is shown to be tautological, one can try to assemble the pieces of the boundary using Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 2.4 (Lemma 4.1 of []). ‣ 2.1. Comparing Chow of 
          
            
              
              
                ¯
                M
              
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  g
                  ,
                  n
                
              
            
          
         and 
          
            
              
              M
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  g
                  ,
                  n ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>. However, it is not necessary that <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g^{\prime}+n^{\prime}\leq 2g+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g^{\prime}+n^{\prime}\leq 2g+n</annotation></semantics></math>.
Indeed, one interesting example is that, although <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math> is infinite-dimensional, we have
<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,9})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,9})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,9})=R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,9})</annotation></semantics></math>. This is proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> by modeling pointed genus <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> curves on <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}\times\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}\times\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and allowing for the possibility that the curves are simply nodal. In this way, we gain a handle on the Chow ring of <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,9}</annotation></semantics></math> minus the boundary divisors with a separating node, and show it is generated by tautological classes. Then, the boundary divisors with a separating node are the images of
gluing maps whose sources are products of smaller moduli spaces with the CKgP whose Chow rings are known to be tautological.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>Non-tautological classes</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Outside of genus <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, the main known source of non-tautological classes are loci of double covers. Let</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{g,2m}=\{C\in\mathcal{M}_{g}:\exists\ \pi:C\to E\text{ for $E$ a genus $1$ curve and $\pi(p_{2i})=\pi(p_{2i-1})$}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mo rspace="0.667em">∃</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mtext> for </mtext><mi>E</mi><mtext> a genus </mtext><mn>1</mn><mtext> curve and </mtext><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{g,2m}=\{C\in\mathcal{M}_{g}:\exists\ \pi:C\to E\text{ for $E$ a genus $1$ curve and $\pi(p_{2i})=\pi(p_{2i-1})$}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">be the locus of pointed bielliptic curves where the marked points are constrained to be conjugate under the degree <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> cover. Write <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{B}}_{g,2m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{B}}_{g,2m}</annotation></semantics></math> for the closure of <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{g,2m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{g,2m}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,2m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,2m}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(van Zelm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmthm5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g+m\geq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g+m\geq 12</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\overline{\mathcal{B}}_{g,2m}]\notin R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,2m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\overline{\mathcal{B}}_{g,2m}]\notin R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,2m})</annotation></semantics></math>. Its image under the cycle class map is also non-tautological.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the case <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g+m=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g+m=12</annotation></semantics></math>, the method for showing these cycles are non-tautological involves pulling back along appropriate gluing maps <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,2m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,2m}</annotation></semantics></math>. The pullback of <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{B}}_{g,2m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{B}}_{g,2m}</annotation></semantics></math> is expressible as a multiple of the diagonal plus some contribution from the boundary. The fact that <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> implies that the diagonal inside <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}</annotation></semantics></math>
has non-tautological Künneth components. However, the pullbacks of tautological classes always have tautological Künneth comonents by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, Appendix A]</cite>.
Because pushforward along forgetful maps is surjective on Chow groups and sends tautological classes to tautological classes, if <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\neq R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\neq R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n+1})\neq R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n+1})\neq R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n+1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, we can draw small red exes in the region <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+n\geq 24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+n\geq 24</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Additional care is needed to show that these bielliptic cycles yield non-tautological classes on the interior <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, represented by large red exes.
These are a consequence of the following theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmthm6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a non-tautological class in <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> whose image in <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is also non-tautological for <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying either of the following conditions</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I2.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I2.ix1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;"> <math id="S3.I2.ix1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000" mathsize="1.200em">×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> 
<div id="S3.I2.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leq g\leq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leq g\leq 12</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+n\geq 24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+n\geq 24</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I2.ix2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;"> <math id="S3.I2.ix2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000" mathsize="1.200em">×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> 
<div id="S3.I2.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 13</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+n\geq 32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+n\geq 32</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In each of these cases, it is shown that the fundamental class of a locus of double covers of curves is non-tautological.
The first case, treated in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> was <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)=(2,20)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)=(2,20)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The cases with <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leq g\leq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leq g\leq 12</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+n=24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+n=24</annotation></semantics></math> were established by van Zelm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>.
In these cases, the non-tautological cycle on <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, these ideas were generalized to double covers of curves of genus <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, establishing the cases with <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is even and <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+n\geq 32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+n\geq 32</annotation></semantics></math>, as well as <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2,3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2,3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is even and <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4\mid 2g+n\geq 32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\mid 2g+n\geq 32</annotation></semantics></math>. The remaining cases, especially with <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> odd, are treated by improving upon these ideas in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, the small red exes, seen in the chart with <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g&lt;12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g&lt;12</annotation></semantics></math>, are cases where <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for some odd <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, which by Corollary <a href="#S2.Thmthm8" title="Corollary 2.8. ‣ 2.2. Comparing Chow and cohomology ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a>, implies that <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\neq R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\neq R^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>).
These odd cohomology classes come from pushing forward classes in <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math> along gluing maps. One can verify that these push forwards are non-zero by pulling them back along the same gluing map and using the push-pull formula.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.7</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Canning–L.–Payne <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmthm7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 11</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1},\ldots,g_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1},\ldots,g_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be distinct positive integers, and set <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1+g_{1}+\ldots+g_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1+g_{1}+\ldots+g_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11+2k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\neq 0\text{ for $n\geq 11-k$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> for </mtext><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11+2k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\neq 0\text{ for $n\geq 11-k$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The above result implies <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{33}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>33</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{33}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 67" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>67</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 67</annotation></semantics></math>.
We have also used similar ideas to construct odd cohomology classes starting with known non-zero classes in <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{17}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,14})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>17</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{17}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{2,14})</annotation></semantics></math>. This produces odd cohomology classes in genus <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math> with no markings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.8</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Canning–L.–Payne–Willwacher <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmthm8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 16</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{45+2(g-16)}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>45</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{45+2(g-16)}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5. </span>Interpreting the chart in cohomology</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Considering the references we have given in Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4. Non-tautological classes ‣ 3. When are the Chow or cohomology rings tautological? ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, every red ex in the chart is also an instance where <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\neq RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\neq RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, by Corollary <a href="#S2.Thmthm8" title="Corollary 2.8. ‣ 2.2. Comparing Chow and cohomology ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a>, every filled circle in the chart is an instance where <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.
There are a few further instances where all cohomology is known to be tautological. For the pairs <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math> listed in the proposition below, this is a slightly stronger statement than in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Theorem 8]</cite>, where it is shown that all even degree cohomology is tautological. We defer the proof to the end of Section <a href="#S5" title="5. Applications to point counts ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> as it weaves together several results explained in the next two sections.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.9</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have <math id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for the following pairs of <math id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3,9)\qquad(4,7)\qquad(5,5)\qquad(6,3)\qquad(7,1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3,9)\qquad(4,7)\qquad(5,5)\qquad(6,3)\qquad(7,1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.10</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although the references given in Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3. Tautological classes in genus 2 through 9 ‣ 3. When are the Chow or cohomology rings tautological? ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> for <math id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)=(2,0),(2,1),(3,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)=(2,0),(2,1),(3,0)</annotation></semantics></math> provide a presentation of the ring, in general even when
<math id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, we do not necessarily understand the cohomology ring, since the tautological ring is not known.
However,
the fact that <math id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> together with Poincaré duality implies that the pairing in the tautological ring is perfect. Moreover, there are known algorithms to compute the pairing in the tautological ring, which would then determine all relations (a class is zero if and only if it pairs to zero with all classes in complimentary degree). Such calculations are very computationally expensive, but progress in this direction, in particular determining the Betti numbers when <math id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 6</annotation></semantics></math>, is the subject of forthcoming work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.11</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The reader is warned that although open circles imply the <em id="S3.Thmthm11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pure weight</em> cohomology of <math id="S3.Thmthm11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological by Remark <a href="#S3.Thmthm1" title="Remark 3.1. ‣ 3. When are the Chow or cohomology rings tautological? ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm10" title="Lemma 2.10 (Lemma 4.3 of []). ‣ 2.2. Comparing Chow and cohomology ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.10</span></a>, they do not imply <math id="S3.Thmthm11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=RH^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=RH^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>. For example <math id="S3.Thmthm11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{0,4})\neq RH^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{0,4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{0,4})\neq RH^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{0,4})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.6. </span>The Madsen–Weiss theorem</h3>

<div id="S3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We end this section with a celebrated result about the cohomology of the interior <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and its tautological ring. Although we rarely expect the entire cohomology ring of <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> to be tautological, the Madsen–Weiss theorem says that the cohomology groups in low degrees relative to <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> are all tautological. The extension to marked points is due to Looijenga. The precise range where the cohomology groups become tautological has improved over the years. The statement we give below can be found in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, Theorem 1.1]</cite>.
Recall that by excision and our definition of the tautological ring in the introduction, <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by the psi and kappa classes. Thus, <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by their images under the cycle class map.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.12</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Harer, Madsen–Weiss, Looijenga)<span id="S3.Thmthm12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}[\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2},\ldots,\psi_{1},\ldots,\psi_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}[\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2},\ldots,\psi_{1},\ldots,\psi_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> be the free polynomial algebra with generators <math id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{1},\ldots,\psi_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{1},\ldots,\psi_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> in degree <math id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> in degree <math id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2j</annotation></semantics></math>. The map</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}[\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2},\ldots,\psi_{1},\ldots,\psi_{n}]\to H^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}[\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2},\ldots,\psi_{1},\ldots,\psi_{n}]\to H^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is an isomorphism in degrees <math id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="*\leq\frac{2}{3}g-\frac{2}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">*\leq\frac{2}{3}g-\frac{2}{3}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Cohomology in low weights</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As we have seen in the previous section, for <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, there are only finitely many moduli spaces <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> whose entire cohomology ring is tautological. Nevertheless, we may restrict to a given cohomological degree <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and ask: Is <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> tautological, and if not, can we give generators?
The first results in this direction are due to Arbarello and Cornalba in the 1990s.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Arbarello–Cornalba <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>)<span id="S4.Thmthm1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0,1,2,3,5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0,1,2,3,5</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The tautological ring vanishes in odd degrees, so
when <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is odd, the theorem simply says that <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=0</annotation></semantics></math>. The most difficult case of the theorem is when <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, Arbarello and Cornalba provide a basis for <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{2}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{2}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, there are no relations among the generators <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{1},\psi_{1},\ldots,\psi_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{1},\psi_{1},\ldots,\psi_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the fundamental classes of boundary strata. For <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, explicit relations are given, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Theorem 2.2]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Arbarello and Cornalba’s proof utilizes pullbacks to the boundary to set up an inductive argument. The essential lemma is the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Lemma 2.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, Proposition 2.1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>)<span id="S4.Thmthm2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Define</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(g,n)=\begin{cases}n-4&amp;\text{if $g=0$}\\
2g-2+n&amp;\text{if $g&gt;0$ and $n\geq 2$}\\
2g.&amp;\text{if $g&gt;0$ and $n=0,1$.}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> and </mtext><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> and </mtext><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">.</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(g,n)=\begin{cases}n-4&amp;\text{if $g=0$}\\
2g-2+n&amp;\text{if $g&gt;0$ and $n\geq 2$}\\
2g.&amp;\text{if $g&gt;0$ and $n=0,1$.}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(g,n)&gt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(g,n)&gt;k</annotation></semantics></math>, then the pullback map <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\to H^{k}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\to H^{k}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}})</annotation></semantics></math> is injective.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To see the utility of the lemma, we sketch how it can be used prove the vanishing of low-degree odd cohomology groups inductively. Fix some odd integer <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that we know <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k^{\prime}}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k^{\prime}}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for odd <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\prime}\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\prime}\leq k</annotation></semantics></math> and the finitely many <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(g^{\prime},n^{\prime})\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(g^{\prime},n^{\prime})\leq k</annotation></semantics></math>. These are the base cases of the induction. Now suppose we are given some <math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g,n</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(g,n)&gt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(g,n)&gt;k</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 4.2 (Lemma 2.5 [], Proposition 2.1 []). ‣ 4. Cohomology in low weights ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, we have an injection</p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\hookrightarrow\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em" stretchy="false">↪</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\hookrightarrow\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H^{k}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}})=H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,n+2})\oplus\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}A_{1}\sqcup A_{2}=\{1,\ldots,n\}\\
g_{1}+g_{2}=g\end{subarray}}H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},A_{1}\cup p}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},A_{2}\cup p^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊔</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H^{k}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal{M}_{g,n}})=H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,n+2})\oplus\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}A_{1}\sqcup A_{2}=\{1,\ldots,n\}\\
g_{1}+g_{2}=g\end{subarray}}H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},A_{1}\cup p}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},A_{2}\cup p^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,n+2})\oplus\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}A_{1}\sqcup A_{2}=\{1,\ldots,n\}\\
g_{1}+g_{2}=g\end{subarray}}\bigoplus_{k_{1}+k_{2}=k}H^{k_{1}}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},A_{1}\cup p})\otimes H^{k_{2}}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},A_{2}\cup p^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊔</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,n+2})\oplus\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}A_{1}\sqcup A_{2}=\{1,\ldots,n\}\\
g_{1}+g_{2}=g\end{subarray}}\bigoplus_{k_{1}+k_{2}=k}H^{k_{1}}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{1},A_{1}\cup p})\otimes H^{k_{2}}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{2},A_{2}\cup p^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where we have used the Künneth formula in the second row. By induction on <math id="S4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, we may assume <math id="S4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,n+2})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,n+2})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is odd, one of <math id="S4.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is odd, say <math id="S4.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Additionally, either <math id="S4.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}&lt;g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}&lt;g</annotation></semantics></math> or if <math id="S4.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}=g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}=g</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S4.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A_{i}|+1&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A_{i}|+1&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, by induction, we may assume that <math id="S4.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k_{i}}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{i},A_{i}\cup p})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k_{i}}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{i},A_{i}\cup p})=0</annotation></semantics></math>. This shows that the right-hand side vanishes. Since the map is injective, we conclude that <math id="S4.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In order to improve upon Arbarello and Cornalba’s results and run this strategy for odd values of <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> greater than <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>, more base cases were needed, particularly the cases <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, Bergström–Faber–Payne proved the required odd cohomology vanishing for these base cases via point counting. They use models of pointed genus <math id="S4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> cures as complete intersections of a quadric and a cubic in <math id="S4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and an intricate sieve to count the smooth such intersections. In the end, they find that point count is given by a polynomial in <math id="S4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm16" title="Theorem 2.16 (van den Bogaart–Edixhoven []). ‣ 2.4. Comparing cohomology and point counts ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.16</span></a>, it follows that the odd cohomology groups of <math id="S4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{4,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{4,n}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math> vanish and the even cohomology groups are pure Tate, with ranks given by corresponding coefficients of the polynomial. Using Arbarello and Cornalba’s inductive method, they obtain the following theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Bergström–Faber–Payne <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>)<span id="S4.Thmthm3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=7,9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=7,9</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The results of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> give an alternative approach to
proving the vanishing of odd cohomology for the base cases. Indeed, for every <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(g,n)\leq 9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(g,n)\leq 9</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a filled circle in the chart in Section <a href="#S3" title="3. When are the Chow or cohomology rings tautological? ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Thus, by Corollary <a href="#S2.Thmthm8" title="Corollary 2.8. ‣ 2.2. Comparing Chow and cohomology ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a>, <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, so all odd cohomology vanishes. Note however, that the genus <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> cases lie beyond the range where computational techniques can determine the ranks of <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, so this method does not determine the exact polynomial for the point counts, as was done in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> for <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=11</annotation></semantics></math>, as we have seen earlier, <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, so there will be no uniform vanishing result. Nevertheless, for all <math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)\neq(1,11)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)\neq(1,11)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(g,n)\leq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(g,n)\leq 11</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a filled circle in the chart in Section <a href="#S3" title="3. When are the Chow or cohomology rings tautological? ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, which means in particular that all odd cohomology vanishes. Thus, the only non-vanishing odd cohomology group in the base case region is <math id="S4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math>. The following result is proved using improvements upon Arbarello and Cornalba’s original induction. Below, for a partition <math id="S4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the associated irreducible <math id="S4.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-representation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.5</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Canning–L.–Payne <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>)<span id="S4.Thmthm5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> unless <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 11</annotation></semantics></math> in which case <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by pullbacks along the forgetful maps <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11}</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, we have an <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant isomorphism of Galois representations</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})=V_{n-10,1^{10}}\otimes H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})=V_{n-10,1^{10}}\otimes\mathsf{S}_{12}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,n})=V_{n-10,1^{10}}\otimes H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})=V_{n-10,1^{10}}\otimes\mathsf{S}_{12}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Continuing on to <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=13</annotation></semantics></math>, there are several <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(g,n)\leq 13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(g,n)\leq 13</annotation></semantics></math> that lie beyond the filled circles in Section <a href="#S3" title="3. When are the Chow or cohomology rings tautological? ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Nevertheless, for every <math id="S4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(g,n)\leq 13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(g,n)\leq 13</annotation></semantics></math> and no filled circle, we have either <math id="S4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math> or an open circle at <math id="S4.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S4.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math> work of Petersen shows that all degree <math id="S4.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>13</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">13</annotation></semantics></math> cohomology is pushed forward from the boundary. For the cases with <math id="S4.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, the presence of an open circle together with Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm10" title="Lemma 2.10 (Lemma 4.3 of []). ‣ 2.2. Comparing Chow and cohomology ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.10</span></a>, also implies that all degree <math id="S4.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>13</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">13</annotation></semantics></math> cohomology is pushed forward from the boundary.
Since <math id="S4.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}</annotation></semantics></math> is understood by Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 4.5 (Canning–L.–Payne []). ‣ 4. Cohomology in low weights ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>, this gives adequate understanding of these base cases to run an inductive argument in the style of Arbarello and Cornalba’s <math id="S4.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math> case.
This succeeds in determining <math id="S4.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> with generators and relations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.6</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Canning–L.–Payne–Willwacher <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>)<span id="S4.Thmthm6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The cohomology group <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is spanned by the images of the pushforward maps</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{A}:H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A\cup p})\otimes H^{0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,A^{c}\cup p^{\prime}})\rightarrow H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{A}:H^{11}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,A\cup p})\otimes H^{0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,A^{c}\cup p^{\prime}})\rightarrow H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> then the sum <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bigoplus_{A}\iota_{A*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⨁</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>A</mi></msub><mi>ι</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{A}\iota_{A*}</annotation></semantics></math> is also injective. In fact, for <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, we have an <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant isomorphism of Galois representations</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=\left(\bigoplus_{m=10}^{n}\mathrm{Ind}_{\mathbb{S}_{m}\times\mathbb{S}_{n-m}}^{\mathbb{S}_{n}}\left((V_{m-9,1^{9}}\oplus V_{m-10,1^{10}})\boxtimes\mathbf{1}\right)\right)\otimes\mathsf{LS}_{12}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>13</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>Ind</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>9</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊠</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>𝖫𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=\left(\bigoplus_{m=10}^{n}\mathrm{Ind}_{\mathbb{S}_{m}\times\mathbb{S}_{n-m}}^{\mathbb{S}_{n}}\left((V_{m-9,1^{9}}\oplus V_{m-10,1^{10}})\boxtimes\mathbf{1}\right)\right)\otimes\mathsf{LS}_{12}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Although similarly explicit presentations of <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> are not known for other values of <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>,
using variations on Arbarello and Cornalba’s induction together with the chart in Section <a href="#S3" title="3. When are the Chow or cohomology rings tautological? ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> to treat bases cases,
Conjecture <a href="#S2.Thmthm20" title="Conjecture 2.20. ‣ 2.4. Comparing cohomology and point counts ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.20</span></a> has been confirmed for <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 15</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.7</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Canning–L.–Payne <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>)<span id="S4.Thmthm7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, and for any <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 15</annotation></semantics></math>, we have isomorphisms of Galois representations</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})^{\mathrm{ss}}=\begin{cases}\bigoplus\mathsf{L}^{k/2}&amp;\text{if $k$ even}\\
\bigoplus\mathsf{S}_{12}&amp;\text{if $k=11$}\\
\bigoplus\mathsf{LS}_{12}&amp;\text{if $k=13$}\\
\bigoplus\mathsf{L^{2}S}_{12}\oplus\bigoplus\mathsf{S}_{16}&amp;\text{if $k=15$}\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ss</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>⨁</mo><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mi>k</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> even</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>⨁</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>⨁</mo><msub><mi>𝖫𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>𝟤</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>⊕</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>⨁</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>16</mn></msub></mrow></mstyle></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">otherwise</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})^{\mathrm{ss}}=\begin{cases}\bigoplus\mathsf{L}^{k/2}&amp;\text{if $k$ even}\\
\bigoplus\mathsf{S}_{12}&amp;\text{if $k=11$}\\
\bigoplus\mathsf{LS}_{12}&amp;\text{if $k=13$}\\
\bigoplus\mathsf{L^{2}S}_{12}\oplus\bigoplus\mathsf{S}_{16}&amp;\text{if $k=15$}\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">At present, we do not have sufficient information about base cases needed to push Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 4.7 (Canning–L.–Payne []). ‣ 4. Cohomology in low weights ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a> beyond weight <math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math>. However, known rationality results for small moduli spaces (which imply vanishing of the spaces of holomorphic forms) have enabled a description of
the Hodge component <math id="S4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{17,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>17</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{17,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> and, conditional upon the conjectured vanishing of some spaces of holomorphic forms in genus <math id="S4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, a description of <math id="S4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{19,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>19</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{19,0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> as well, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Conjecture <a href="#S2.Thmthm20" title="Conjecture 2.20. ‣ 2.4. Comparing cohomology and point counts ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.20</span></a> predicts that for even <math id="S4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 20</annotation></semantics></math>, all cohomology of <math id="S4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is pure Tate. By the Tate conjecture, such classes are expected to be algebraic. Meanwhile, all known examples of non-tautological algebraic cycles, such as those in Theorems <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 3.5 (van Zelm []). ‣ 3.4. Non-tautological classes ‣ 3. When are the Chow or cohomology rings tautological? ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmthm6" title="Theorem 3.6 (see []). ‣ 3.4. Non-tautological classes ‣ 3. When are the Chow or cohomology rings tautological? ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>,
occur in degrees <math id="S4.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>22</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 22</annotation></semantics></math>. This suggests the following conjecutre.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conj">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 4.8</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For even <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 20</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g,n</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The conjecture is known to hold for <math id="S4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Theorem 1.1]</cite>, for <math id="S4.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=4</annotation></semantics></math>, and for <math id="S4.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=6</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S4.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 10</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, Theorem 1.5(1) and (2)]</cite>. The conjecture is also known when <math id="S4.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> (see Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 3.4 (Petersen []). ‣ 3.2. Genus 1 ‣ 3. When are the Chow or cohomology rings tautological? ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>) and when <math id="S4.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, Corollary 2.7]</cite>.
By Hard Lefschetz, Conjecture <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Conjecture 4.8. ‣ 4. Cohomology in low weights ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a> would also imply that <math id="S4.p11.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\mathrel{\mathchoice{\vbox{\hbox{$\displaystyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\textstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle:$}}}{=}}H^{2(3g-3+n)-k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>:</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>=</mo></mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\mathrel{\mathchoice{\vbox{\hbox{$\displaystyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\textstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptstyle:$}}}{\vbox{\hbox{$\scriptscriptstyle:$}}}{=}}H^{2(3g-3+n)-k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological for even <math id="S4.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 20</annotation></semantics></math>. As further evidence towards Conjecture <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Conjecture 4.8. ‣ 4. Cohomology in low weights ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, Theorem 1.5(3)]</cite> shows that <math id="S4.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for even <math id="S4.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 14</annotation></semantics></math>. In these cases where <math id="S4.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=RH_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, Poincaré duality shows that
Conjecture <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Conjecture 4.8. ‣ 4. Cohomology in low weights ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a> holds if and only the pairing <math id="S4.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RH^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\times RH_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\to\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RH^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\times RH_{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\to\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is perfect.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Low weight compactly supported cohomology of <math id="S4.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Once <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is understood for all <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, one might hope to use Deligne’s weight spectral sequence to calculate <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{k}H_{c}^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{k}H_{c}^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> as discussed in Section <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3. Comparing cohomology of 
          
            
              
              
                ¯
                M
              
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  g
                  ,
                  n
                
              
            
          
         and 
          
            
              
              M
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  g
                  ,
                  n ‣ 2. Relationships between the different invariants ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>.
Since <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})=\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the weight <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> cohomology reflects purely combinatorial aspects of the boundary stratification.
This idea was pioneered in the case <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0</annotation></semantics></math> by Chan–Galatius–Payne <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, where they find that <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{0}H_{c}^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=W_{0}H^{*}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{0}H_{c}^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})=W_{0}H^{*}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is computed by the cohomology of the genus <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> part of the standard commutative graph complex with <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> marked external vertices. Dualizing, they use this to prove at least exponential growth of the top weight cohomology of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Building upon Theorems <a href="#S4.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Arbarello–Cornalba []). ‣ 4. Cohomology in low weights ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> and <a href="#S4.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 4.5 (Canning–L.–Payne []). ‣ 4. Cohomology in low weights ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>, the weight <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> compactly supported cohomology of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=11</annotation></semantics></math> are studied by Payne and Willwacher in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> respectively. Additional progress for <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=11</annotation></semantics></math> was made in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. Finally, building upon Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm6" title="Theorem 4.6 (Canning–L.–Payne–Willwacher []). ‣ 4. Cohomology in low weights ‣ Chow rings, cohomology rings, and point counts of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>, some results on the weight <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=13</annotation></semantics></math> cohomology are given in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, Proposition 5.1]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Applications to point counts</h2>

